Proposition 5.4 (Scott 1981, PRG-19) states that the map sending x to the least fixed point in y of τ(x, y) equals the approximable map recovered from the least fixed point of the recursion operator of τ.

Let g₀ be that least fixed point and G the approximable map it determines. The fixed-point equation of the operator, rewritten by its application law, says that G at x equals τ at the pair of x and G at x. Comparing on elements, the left-hand value at x is the least fixed point of the section at x, while G at x is a fixed point of that section, so the left-hand map lies below G. Conversely, the filter of the left-hand map is a pre-fixed point of the operator: converting the operator value back to a map recovers the left-hand map, by extensionality on elements, the application law, the round trip of the function-space equivalence, and the fixed-point identity of the left-hand map, and the inverse round trip transports that equality to the filters. The least fixed point therefore lies below that filter, and monotonicity of the function-space equivalence, with the same round trip, yields the reverse inequality of maps.
